  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively establish several important components: powder-based additive or in-situ alloy manufacture of refractory HEAs (especially R19), high-temperature powder consolidation, quench-sensitive phase evolution, strong interstitial-oxygen strengthening in BCC refractory alloys including the closely matching Hf-Nb-Ta-Ti-Zr family (R4, R9, R11), and silicide coating formation on a similar refractory HEA (R5). They therefore point toward a printed, sintered BCC refractory alloy using controlled oxygen and a silicide-based oxidation barrier. However, none discloses hydride-containing extrusion ink, hydrogen de-binding, sequential hydride decomposition according to formation enthalpy, titanium-sponge oxygen control, or the specific self-healing oxide-filling mechanism. Combining the records into the later process would consequently require the substantial new idea of hydride-enabled direct-ink processing and its controlled decomposition, so the result is only partially reconstructible."
